For a function component, wrap it with `memo` and use the returned component: [1][2]

```js
const Greeting = memo(function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
});
```

React skips re-rendering it when its props are unchanged, although changes to its own state or used context still trigger a re-render. [1]

For a class component, extend `PureComponent`; it shallowly compares props and state. [5][6]

Treat memoization only as a performance optimization, not as a correctness requirement. [1]

